  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Francis Al\u00ffs. The Nature of the Game shows clear early use as an open-access scholarly book, with 1,489 recorded downloads and repository availability through multiple open instances. Its record also points to policy-relevant resonance through alignment with Sustainable Development Goal 11, Sustainable Cities and Communities, while the funding trail identifies KU Leuven support.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2023 scholarly book by Gerard-Jan Claes and St\u00e9phane Symons examines Francis Al\u00ffs\u2019s long-running Children\u2019s Games project and places it within art-historical, geographic, and social inquiry. The strongest impact signal in the record is usage: the book has been downloaded 1,489 times in OAPEN metrics, suggesting sustained reader interest soon after publication. It is also openly available through several repository instances, which likely helps widen access beyond a single platform.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record shows no indexed citations in OpenAlex yet, so the book\u2019s scholarly citation footprint is still early. That does not diminish the evidence of readership already visible in the download data; rather, it indicates that the book\u2019s longer-term citation trajectory has not yet had time to emerge in the indexed literature. The absence of event data and public-discourse records means there is little formal trace here of conference uptake or media circulation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A modest but meaningful sustainability signal appears in the SDG alignment with Sustainable Cities and Communities. For a book centered on play, movement, and urban space, that alignment is coherent with the work\u2019s broader relevance to how cities are inhabited and imagined. In this sense, the record suggests not only art-historical interest but also a line of relevance for conversations about urban life and community space.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the book\u2019s impact profile is strongest in access and readership rather than citation or public controversy. Open dissemination, repository presence, and steady downloads indicate an audience already engaging with the work, while KU Leuven funding and the open-science record underline institutional support for its circulation. The evidence points to a book with clear accessibility and thematic reach, whose scholarly uptake may continue to develop over time.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
